PRACTICAL – 1
Aim:

Setting up and configuring a Malware analysis Lab (Kali Linux)

Objectives:
Install virtualization software and create a resource optimized Kali Linux VM.
Configure network isolation to prevent malware C2 communication.
Install comprehensive static analysis tools.
Create safe file transfer mechanism and clean baseline snapshot.
Procedure:

(i) Install virtualization software (e.g., VirtualBox) & enable VT-x/AMD-V
(ii) Create a Kali Linux VM with required RAM, CPU & disk
(iii) Install Kali Linux using ISO & verify successful boot
(iv) Configure network isolation (Host-only adapter, disable internet)
(v) Adjust system settings (disable updates, enable file visibility, configure firewall)
(vi) Install malware analysis tools (Wireshark, strings, objdump etc.)
(vii) Create a shared folder for safe file transfer (disable after use)
(viii) Take a clean snapshot of the VM for recovery

Observations:
VM boot cleanly with 4GB RAM, network isolation
All tools are installed for analysis of malware
Shared folder mounted for file transfer
Snapshot taken to restore VM to pre-analysis baseline within 30 seconds
Result:
We have successfully created a secure malware analysis lab
Environment features network isolation, comprehensive toolset, safe file transfer via shared folder, and snapshot-based rapid recovery





PRACTICAL – 2
Aim:

To perform basic static analysis of windows binaries on a Linux platform.

Objectives:
To examine windows binaries without executing them
To extract hidden information such as strings, metadata and structure
To identify suspicious patterns in malware samples
Procedure:

(i) Open terminal in Kali Linux
(ii) Place the windows binary file in the working directory
(iii) Use the following tools:

Check file type

file sample.exe

Extract readable strings

strings sample.exe

View metadata

exiftool sample.exe

Analyze binary structure

binwalk sample.exe

Check dependencies

objdump -x sample.exe
Observations:

Strings reveal readable text like URLs, file paths or commands
exiftool shows metadata
binwalk detects embedded files or packed data
objdump shows headers & sections of the binary

Result:

Basic static analysis of windows binary was successfully performed on Linux without executing the file.





