PRACTICAL – 4
Aim:

To create and apply YARA rules for identifying malware based on patterns and signatures.

Objectives:
To understand the purpose of YARA in malware detection
To install and use YARA in a windows environment
To create a simple YARA rule file
To scan a test file using the created rule
To observe how YARA identifies suspicious content based on matching strings
Procedure:
(i) Installation of YARA

a. Download YARA

Visit official GitHub page of YARA
Download latest release for your OS

b. Install YARA

Extract downloaded file
Add YARA folder to system path

c. Verify installation

Open cmd prompt
Type:
yara --version
Confirm installation is successful
(ii) Writing YARA rules

a. Create rule file

Open text editor
Save file as sample_rule.yar

b. Write basic YARA rule

rule suspicious_string
{
    strings:
        $a = "malware"
        $b = "hack"
        $c = "cmd.exe"

    condition:
        any of them
}

c. Save the file

(iii) Applying YARA rule

a. Run YARA scan

Open command prompt
Execute:
yara rule.yar suspicious_file.exe

b. Analyze output

If matched: rule name will be displayed
If no match: no output
(iv) Advanced rule (optional)

a. Add file size condition

filesize < 1MB and any of them

b. Use multiple conditions

Combine strings & logic for accuracy
Observation:
YARA executed successfully from the command line
The rule file "sample_rule.yar" was created without errors
The test file contained the word "malware"
When the scan command was run, YARA matched the string in the file
The output displayed the rule name and the file name, showing that the rule worked correctly
Result:

The practical was completed successfully. A YARA rule was created and applied to a test file. The tool correctly detected the suspicious strings present in the file, demonstrating how YARA can be used for malware detection based on patterns and signatures.




PRACTICAL – 5
Aim:

To understand how high-level C language constructs are translated into assembly language.

Objectives:
To write a simple C program
To convert the C program into assembly code using GCC
To study how variables, arithmetic operations & function calls appear in assembly
To understand basic stack & register usage during program execution
To relate high-level program logic with low-level machine instructions
Procedure:
(i) Writing C program

a. Create a C program

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 5, b = 10, c;
    c = a + b;
    printf("%d", c);
}

b. Save file

(ii) Convert C to Assembly

a. Compile to assembly

Open terminal
Run:
gcc -S program.c

b. View assembly file

Open generated file program.s
(iii) Analyze assembly code

a. Identify sections

data → variables
text → instructions

b. Understand variable storage

Observe how a, b, c are stored

c. Analyze instructions

Example:

movl $5, -4(%rbp)
movl $10, -8(%rbp)

d. Arithmetic operations

Identify addition:
addl %eax, %edx

e. Function call

Observe printf call in assembly
(iv) Stack & registers

a. Understand stack frame

rbp → base pointer
rsp → stack pointer

b. Trace execution

Follow instructions step-by-step
Observation:
The C program was successfully converted into assembly code
Variable assignments in C were translated into mov instructions
The addition operation was represented using arithmetic instructions such as add
The output function printf() appeared as a function call in assembly
The stack frame showed how local variables were managed using registers like rbp & rsp
The practical helped in understanding the relationship between high-level C statements & low-level instructions
Result:

The practical was completed successfully. The generated assembly code clearly showed how C language constructs such as variable declarations, arithmetic operations & function calls are translated into low-level instructions. This improved understanding of program execution at machine level.







